{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And it shall be when you come, etc.</b> Now the plain sense of the verse indicates that this commandment applies only after their entry into the land. If so, why did HaShem change this intention and command the children of Israel (Numbers 9:1) in the second year to perform the Pesach in its appointed time according to all its statutes?" "<b>And it will be seen</b> that HaShem revealed supernal knowledge in this matter. For it had been in His thought that if Israel merited, they would not perform a second Pesach until [they were] in the land of Canaan. But because of their rebellions from the time they left Egypt — for they were rebels against the mouth of HaShem, and even at the Sea of Reeds they began to rebel, as it is written (Psalms 106:7): ״וַיַּמְרוּ עַל יָם בְּיַם סוּף״ — this was the cause that the days were lengthened and they did not merit to ascend immediately and at once. And therefore when HaShem saw that their deeds caused it and they did not enter the land while they were still in the wilderness, it became necessary to command them to perform the Pesach in the wilderness, for until now they had been commanded only ״כִּי תָבֹאוּ אֶל הָאָרֶץ״. And even though the Master calls the generations from the beginning (Isaiah 41:4), nevertheless His good thoughts He did declare to His people, as mentioned."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:15.692771689Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}